## The Recalls Continue: A Snapshot of Food Safety Failures

Recent statistics show that 433 food recalls have occurred this year, with May contributing a hefty 106 cases. This month's recalls predominantly fall under Class II, with foreign objects and undeclared allergens leading the charge. May's most disturbing entries include glass fragments, plastic shreds, and allergen mislabeling.

### Foreign Objects: The Uninvited Guests
Dakota Honey Company's "Spreadable Spun Honey" variety has been recalled due to stainless steel dust/flakes and plastic shreds. Such contaminants in honey products highlight serious production process lapses. Similarly, Alain MILLIAT's marmalade was flagged for glass fragments, making one ponder the safety practices behind these once-trustworthy brands.

### Mislabeling Missteps
Undeclared allergens remain a stubborn issue. WE R NUTS' "Milk Chocolate Bridge Mix" omitted key allergen disclosures, endangering consumers with soy and cashew allergies. Similar errors from "Frederik's Trail Mix" and "Prospector Popcorn" further showcase the industry's struggles with accurate labeling.

### Salmonella Strikes Again
Sugar Foods LLC's croutons faced a Class I recall due to possible salmonella contamination traced back to their supplier's non-fat milk powder. The potential for widespread harm makes this the most severe recall of the month.

### Industry Repeat Offenders
With 75 repeat offenders so far this year, some brands stand out for their consistent violations. Moonlight Packing Corporation and Albertsons Companies LLC, among others, have multiple Class I recalls, raising questions about their compliance measures.

## The State of Food Safety
The persistence of recalls, particularly involving foreign objects and allergens, underscores a need for stricter quality controls. Manufacturers must prioritize consumer trust over cost-cutting measures to ensure product safety. Without substantial systemic change, these incidents will likely remain a regular feature in the food industry.

## Key Takeaways
- The annual number of recalls continues to rise, with nearly 100 cases in May alone.
- Foreign objects and undeclared allergens are the most common recall reasons.
- Repeat offenders and labeling inaccuracies highlight systemic issues.

Stay informed and protect yourself by keeping up with Shelf Check's daily updates.
